---
/*
  The Technology Blueprint's opening (2026-11-04), after the reference the
  user supplied: an isometric network of the practices' systems, a
  Hairline figure (blueprint-hero.js, network-figure.js), on the right:
  seven keys on plinths (seven since the fourth pass), each with a
  practice's mark on its coin (the homepage's "Practices we've worked
  with"), joined by routes with rounded turns, a signal running along them into the hub; the key under
  the pointer rises and lights its routes. It stands on the Blueprint's own
  dash-dot lattice, a key to a cell, which fades out towards the words.

  Since 2026-11-04 (third pass) the opening is two screens long and the
  network is held through both (the hold: CSS sticky): the first screen is
  the hero, exactly the window, its words on the left in the middle of it,
  a little in from the margin (the heading, the standfirst, the buttons);
  they scroll away as the page does, and the statement comes up on the left
  after them. The network stays exactly where it is all the while (fourth
  pass), in the middle of the room between the nav bar's foot and the
  screen's (blueprint-hero.js); only its keys answer the scroll, lifting a
  little as it goes and settling when it stops.
  When the statement reaches the middle of the screen the hold ends, and
  the statement and the network go on up together, the next section
  ("When Technology Has Direction") following. The statement is set as a
  block, its lines (one per line of the Studio's field) brought to the
  same width (blueprint-hero.js); as it comes, the network's routes light
  together. The red block that stood under the hero is gone.

  The words come from the page's Studio fields (headingField, ledeField,
  statementField); the buttons are the slot (actions). Styles: pages.css
  ("Technology Blueprint hero"). Below 992px nothing is held: the network
  stands over the words, the statement under them.
*/
interface Props {
  label?: string;
  heading?: string;
  lede?: string;
  statement?: string;
  labelField?: string;
  headingField?: string;
  ledeField?: string;
  statementField?: string;
}
const { heading, lede, statement, headingField = "hero.heading", ledeField = "hero.lede", statementField = "hero.statement" } = Astro.props;
---
<section class="bp-hero" data-blueprint-hero>
  <!-- the hold: the network and its ground, a screen tall, held in place
       while the words go by -->
  <div class="bp-hero__hold">
    <!-- the scene: the network on its ground, one layer, still while the
         words go by -->
    <div class="bp-hero__scene" data-bp-scene>
      <div class="bp-hero__ground" data-blueprint-ground data-iso-line="dash" data-iso-ground=".bp-hero__svg" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="bp-hero__figure">
        <div class="bp-hero__stage" data-hairline>
          <svg class="bp-hero__svg" viewBox="0 0 400 320" fill="none" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true"></svg>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- the words, scrolling as the page does: the hero's screen, then the
       statement's -->
  <div class="bp-hero__flow">
    <div class="bp-hero__text">
      <h1 class="bp-hero__heading" data-page-field={headingField}>{heading}</h1>
      {lede && <p class="bp-hero__lede" data-page-field={ledeField}>{lede}</p>}
      <div class="bp-hero__actions"><slot name="actions" /></div>
    </div>
    {statement && (
    <div class="bp-hero__next">
      <p class="bp-hero__statement" data-page-field={statementField} data-bp-statement>{statement}</p>
    </div>
    )}
  </div>
</section>
